ny people is is a real  challenge coming into directing that if  you have a literary background or or a  story background you might not  you know be conversant with how  images work and how they work on people  and all that and  you know what has been my salvation  there as my teacher there is again  asking how does this make me feel and  what subjective state do i want to  create in the viewer to best prepare  them to experience the story  if it's if it's a magnificent beauty  shot because i want a romantic feeling  i'll do that if it's if i want to  contextualize a scene between two  characters  that they are small in comparison to  this environment i might do a  magnificent you know put them small in  the frame and see a beautiful vista shot  but if it's really more about  what's this dynamic between these  characters my master may only just be  edge to edge with two characters in it  but it's what again what story are you  telling  did you start your meditation practice  before directing or the other way around  uh well let's see i started uh  no i would say in earnest i started it  after i started i've been doing this for  decades so it's a long time and you know  i think we arrive at what we need as we  go through life at different times and  often in my experience has been that  you know it's taken me often several  decades to get to a place of having any  kind of wisdom about anything at all  so you know i think  being able to just settle down and  detach from  who i take myself who i took myself to  be who i continue to take myself to be  and understand that that's a structure  that's a construct that whoever i really  am is independent of uh of any structure  and that's that's really one of the  things i love about directing is it it  really requires me to be as present as  possible and to be as present as  possible i find is to  be willing to relinquish our ideas about  ourselves how we're perceived how we  perceive ourselves you have to just let  go of t